  {
    "episode_id": 44607,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/4/44607.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 6,
    "turn_text": "You know, I knew we'd cover a lot of issues, but I'm absolutely gobsmacked that we're already talking about Kevin Costner. So there's a first. But I'd love to talk to you about immigration. And this is an issue that's actually very important to the subscribers of a shot in the arm. So trying not to be too controversial, immigration can be a driver of disease and health, but perhaps not in the way that the press think. It's not that immigrants themselves bring disease with them, but if we seek to contain them, we put them at risk at all sorts of communicable and non-communicable diseases that are found already in our societies, in our communities, the very communities they are looking to for help. And you made a comment very early on in the campaign that deeply inspired me. You said, we have an immigration problem. We don't have enough immigrants. You're from immigrant parents yourself. What did you mean by that extraordinary comment?",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
