  {
    "episode_id": 44607,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/4/44607.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 7,
    "turn_text": "You know, immigrants have all been the new vitality, the lifeblood of this country. And in a district like the 21st, where it's the number one, the number on industry is agriculture, dairies, farming, crops, especially crops and so on. And who does all that work? Immigrants, all right? And as you know, to a very, very large degree, over 50%, 60%, some people say 70 or 80%. These are largely undocumented immigrants. And they, As you know, they certainly don't get the respect and the recognition they deserve. I was recently at a cherry harvest, and it is something to see men and women of all ages in Nigeria. It's 50, 50 men and women who were running, literally running up and down ladders, running to dump their bins of cherries in order to be able to make as much money as possible in the shortest amount of time. When was the last time that you saw anybody running as a portion of their work, right? I can say that the immigrants that contribute to not only our industry but to our communities, those are such a great investment. They're not a burden at all. You touched on it a little bit. You know, this public charge issue about the administration, this administration going after any immigrant that could be considered a public charge or may use some type of the public services that are available to everyone. That is having a real harm in public health is causing people not to seek the healthcare that they need to keep themselves healthy and their community's healthy.",
