  {
    "episode_id": 44607,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/4/4/44607.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 13,
    "turn_text": "You know, this is what you need to know about me, that I truly believe it's my base principle that healthcare for all is a human right. All right, just like education, just like clean air, just like clean water. And whether or not it's a one system or another to provide that quality of affordable health care to everyone that needs it is just critical. We know that there's a number of things that we need to do. One, just like my wife says, when she has a critical ear patient, stabilize the patient, find an airway. So we need to stabilize the ACA. We need to get down to price her prescription drugs, and we've done that with some great legislation already this year. And then really is what I've been doing, expand healthcare, you know, rural access through a lot of the different technologies that are available today. Yep. I could tell you, before I was married, It was such a fine example. You may know that I used to work overseas quite a bit, all over Africa, the Middle East, and so on. But on a trip back, I used to fly through London all the time. And my wife, Kathy, she wasn't my wife at that time, but she met me for a little vacation that we were spending in the UK. It was fabulous. But she had a bit of an infection, and we had to go to an ER. And we found a clinic down the street, someplace in London, they took us in. So how do you do? They gave her an examination, they gave her a prescription, and on the way out, do we owe you anything? No. Thank you very much, right? People were ill, they sick, they needed the medicine, they were taken care of, and there you go. That's a civilized society. That's what we should be doing.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_01",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
